 @xTurtleOW  Watch this video: https://youtu.be/LuhLADcx2s8

Sure, the 2070 super is like 5% slower on average, in DX11 games. However, it makes up ground against the 1080 ti in games that leverage DX12, because Turing is just a more modern architecture.

Also, the 2070 super has access to DLSS, which is great, for obvious reasons.

 @vishnuravi8910   The FPS counter do not lie ... The RTX2080ti and 1080ti is in reality so close in performance in most games that a combination of factors can cross the performance gap (because it is so small). Also if you cherry pick your games used for testing you can get any result you want... If you do a little googling you can find benchmark that show the average of I think it was 80 games clearly showing how little the gap is. Sorry but you have been scammed by the corporate propaganda...

I have now sold my GTX1080ti for 700$ same as I did pay for it 3 years ago. Got a RTX3060 now, almost same performance in games, faster doing video upscaling using AI and half the power usage (going solar power of grid in camper van soon and need to save power). 

I miss my GTX1080ti space heater as it has kept my room warm :) But soon it is summer and the heating it provided is not needed anymore.

 @a64738  The 2080 Super, fully stock, has a performance advantage over the 1080ti. Even the normal 2080 performs several percent higher than it. Granted, the 2080 isn’t exactly the best bang for the buck, but the Turing architecture just holds quite a few advancements over Pascal. I highly doubt that a 1080ti founders edition equals the performance of the 2080ti. The 2080ti has more cores, DLSS optimization, faster memory in terms of GDDR6 being approx. 33% faster than GDDR5X in terms of address transporting, and higher 64-bit floating point speeds.

My first source: https://gpu.userbenchmark.com/Compare/Nvidia-RTX-2080-Ti-vs-Nvidia-GTX-1080-Ti/4027vs3918
My second source: https://www.gamesradar.com/gddr6-memory-explained/

